Here's the design, in two sections. Section one: structure and data model.

## 1. Module boundaries

The hard rule is that `domain/` imports nothing from `ui/`, touches no browser API, and takes its clock and storage by injection. That's what makes notification rules testable without a DOM and what makes the eventual server port a matter of swapping the storage adapter.

```
src/
  domain/                 # pure TS — no DOM, no localStorage, no Date.now()
    task.ts               # Task type + command functions (create, edit, complete…)
    events.ts             # TaskEvent union + envelope
    log.ts                # append, since(watermark), byTask
    subscriptions.ts      # resolve(user, task) → watching | muted | auto
    rules.ts              # which event types notify  ← the tunable filter
    notifications.ts      # derive(user, log, subs, watermark) → Notification[]
    clock.ts              # Clock interface (injected; due dates need determinism)
  store/
    storage.ts            # StorageAdapter interface
    local-storage.ts      # today's impl — swap for an HTTP adapter later
  ui/                     # thin; renders domain output, dispatches commands
  main.ts                 # composition root: wires clock + storage + UI
```

`rules.ts` existing as its own module is the payoff of approach A — your "status + due date" choice lives in one file of predicates, and changing it re-derives history rather than requiring a migration.

## 2. Data model

```ts
interface Task {
  id: TaskId;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  status: 'open' | 'done';
  dueAt: string | null;        // ISO-8601 UTC; rendered in local time
  assigneeId: UserId | null;   // exists now, unused until multi-user
  createdBy: UserId;
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
}

interface EventEnvelope {
  id: string;        // monotonic, sortable
  taskId: TaskId;
  actorId: UserId;   // who did it — the basis for "don't notify me about me"
  at: string;
  event: TaskEvent;
}

type TaskEvent =
  | { type: 'task.created' }
  | { type: 'task.status_changed'; from: Status; to: Status }
  | { type: 'task.due_changed';    from: string | null; to: string | null }
  | { type: 'task.assigned';       from: UserId | null; to: UserId | null }
  | { type: 'task.edited';         fields: ('title' | 'description')[] }
  | { type: 'task.due_reached';    threshold: 'due_soon' | 'overdue' }
  | { type: 'task.deleted' };

interface Subscription {
  taskId: TaskId;
  userId: UserId;
  state: 'watching' | 'muted' | 'auto';   // auto = infer from involvement
  updatedAt: string;
}
```

Three deliberate choices worth challenging:

- **`assigneeId` exists now but goes unused.** It's the cheapest possible hedge for the multi-user phase — adding a field to a stored model later means migrating every record.
- **`state: 'auto'` rather than a row per involvement.** Involvement is recomputed from the log (you created, were assigned, or edited it), so `auto` means "no explicit opinion, ask the log." Rows get written only when you actually watch or mute. This is what keeps "both, with implicit defaults" from requiring a subscription row for every task you ever touch.
- **`task.due_reached` is a real logged event**, not a computed-at-render condition. A scanner appends it once when a threshold is crossed, guarded against duplicates. That buys uniform derivation — every notification comes from the log, so dedupe is structural rather than a separate "already alerted?" flag.

Does this structure look right before I go on to the notification pipeline, error handling, and testing?
